WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.440
Is it still possible for young people to&nbsp;
flourish in America? Big questions like this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:15.200
usually make us think of top-down answers: "Kids&nbsp;
can't get ahead because capitalism is broken;"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:20.640
"Marriages fail because the media is setting a bad&nbsp;
example." But psychologist Urie Bronfenbrenner's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.520
theory of human development tells a different&nbsp;
story. He found that children grow and make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:31.280
decisions within a layered system of relationships&nbsp;
and that those relationships with the closest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:37.280
proximity to a child, like families, teachers,&nbsp;
and strong faith communities, have the greatest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:44.240
influence. Other institutions are important but&nbsp;
distant. Government media and the larger society&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:49.440
matter less when the core is strong.&nbsp;
If we want to truly empower children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:55.840
we should partner with the people closest to them — giving&nbsp;
parents the power to choose great schools;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:02.400
teaching kids that the family they form matters;&nbsp;
or helping young people recognize the value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.449
of a personal faith commitment. Every single&nbsp;
effort helps set them up for future success.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.800
To learn more about Urie Bronfenbrenner's&nbsp;
theory of human development, check out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:20.080
links in the description below. Also, let us&nbsp;
know what other topics you'd like our scholars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:27.600
to cover in 60 seconds and be sure to like and&nbsp;
subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

